WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:06.320
I've read many great business books, but recently
people kept telling me, "Pat, can you just

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.330
explain your top 10 books of all time in entrepreneurship?"

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.250
So that's exactly what I'm doing in this video.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.500
The top 10 best books that I believe every
entrepreneur ought to read and why.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.800
I may read a thing or two from the book to
explain why the book is so necessary for entrepreneurs

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.800
to read.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:22.800
Now keep in mind, there are a lot of great
books.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.349
I've got a lot of great books that I wanted
to put on the list but there's only 10 of

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:26.750
them that made it.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:35.280
So, #10 on this list, the #10 book, why I
believe every entrepreneur needs to read this

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.120
is a book by a good friend of mine, Robert
Greene, who's written The 40 Laws of Power,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.600
33, Mastery, Art of Seduction, 50th Law, and
he's working on another one.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.300
He and I were talking last week, that's going
to be unbelievable, similar to the 40 Laws

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.300
of Power.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.179
The number 10 book on my list is The 33 Strategies
of War by Robert Greene.

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:55.179
And I'll tell you why.

00:00:55.179 --> 00:00:57.730
Some people ask, "Why do you talk about war?

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:59.260
What does this have to do with entrepreneurship?

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.749
And I'll explain to you exactly why.

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:05.780
Listen, think about running a business, as
running a small tribe.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.110
You've heard of Tribal Leadership, which is
#11 by the way, but you're running a small

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.110
little tribe.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.400
In other words, you're running a country,
right?

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.600
And when you're running a country, and you're
small, getting started, typically, no one's

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.009
going to try to take over America or Russia,
maybe.

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:26.000
Or a major, major country.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.880
Unless you're some crazy guy from Germany,
than that's a different story, but most of

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.550
the time, people don't go after the bigger
countries.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.799
They make the bigger countries the last to
go after.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:38.250
They start off by taking out small countries.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.990
When you're an entrepreneur, you are running
a very, very small country.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.030
And everybody out there has one goal.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.290
Every competitor of yours.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.990
They want to put you out of business -- if
you're a direct competitor.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:53.119
Not a sideline, not somebody they can partner
up with and you can cross pollinate and they

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:56.040
sell your product and you sell their product,
or partnership on software.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.179
If they're not somebody that can be a vendor
or a partner, they want you to go out of business.

00:02:00.179 --> 00:02:01.179
Think about that.

00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:06.280
So if you don't know the different strategies
they're going to use to put you out of business,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.400
you're going to be caught in a way that you
say, "What the heck just happened?"

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.400
These are pros.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.430
These are professionals.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.730
You're not dealing with lightweights or like,
"oh, it's so great, let us also help you get

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.190
into business and make it."

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.190
No.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.130
Their goal is for you to go out of business.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.130
Period.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:24.540
So you've got to have a mindset of a general,
a warrior, a strategist, somebody that's going

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.860
to be thinking like the Alexander or Napoleon,
so you know when someone's maneuvering a certain

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.160
way, you'll understand what's happening over
there and here's what we're going to do over

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.580
here to have an edge for yourself.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.580
Okay.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.080
Let me read a part of this book here.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.410
My favorite strategy in this book is #4.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:43.320
He says, "Create a sense of urgency and desperation.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.910
He calls it the death ground strategy."

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:46.980
Let me read it to you.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.160
"You are your worst enemy.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:53.400
You waste precious time dreaming of the future
instead of engaging in the present.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.540
Since nothing seems so urgent to you, you
are only half involved in what you do.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:04.960
The only way to change is through action and
outside pressure.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.720
Put yourself in situations where you have
way too much at stake to waste time or resources.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.080
If you cannot afford to lose, you won't.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.040
Cut your ties to the past.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.150
Enter unknown territory where you must be
depending on your wits and energy to see you

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.150
through.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.930
Place yourself on death ground, where your
back is against the wall and you have to fight

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.160
like hell to get out alive."

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.400
See, I like that mindset.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.630
And it goes into explaining what other people
thought that way or did it that way that helped

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:38.090
them get out of it and going out there and
winning, so The #10 book on my list is 33

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:39.200
Strategies of War.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.301
Lots of great strategies in there.

00:03:40.301 --> 00:03:51.670
#9 - The #9 book is an author I heard 14 years
ago, I heard him speak, and I think I heard

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:55.040
him speak in either San Jose or Palm Springs.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.510
I heard this man speak, Michael Gerber.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.840
He wrote a book called, The E Myth.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.750
And the reason why The E Myth is a very, very
important book to read is because it allows

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:09.680
you as the person who calls themself the entrepreneur
to realize that maybe you're really not an

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:10.680
entrepreneur.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.650
You think you're an entrepreneur, but you're
not really an entrepreneur.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:14.650
Right?

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:19.250
Or, you are an entrepreneur, but you're not
paying attention to the other sides of the

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.030
business which is the systems side.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.970
Because a lot of times the entrepreneur may
be the visionary and not want to deal with

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.889
all the minutia, and just want to go out there
and do all this stuff, and they're not really

00:04:27.889 --> 00:04:32.430
paying attention to systems and so you can't
really scale your business.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:33.629
He talks about that in the book.

00:04:33.629 --> 00:04:35.349
I'll read a part of this book to you.

00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:39.410
It says, "If your business is having problems,
in all likelihood, the problem has never been

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:40.410
your business.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.400
It has always been you.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.629
Perhaps you see yourself as an entrepreneur
but work more like a technician.

00:04:46.629 --> 00:04:51.169
To clarify what we mean by this, let's have
a look at the two definitions.

00:04:51.169 --> 00:04:52.169
Entrepreneur.

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:54.439
A person who has vision and dreams of a better
future.

00:04:54.439 --> 00:04:57.080
They innovate, create and practice forward
thinking.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.080
Technician.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.550
A person how is a doer, a worker, a person
who isn't interested in ideas, but actions.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:09.710
Now, when a technician -- listen -- when a
technician's bored of making money for their

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:15.550
boss and they decide to start their own business,
they fail because they see a business as a

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:16.880
place to go to work.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.090
It's just another job.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:22.319
That's why a lot of people fail as an entrepreneur,
because they're really technicians.

00:05:22.319 --> 00:05:28.280
But instead of doing one job, the job they've
always done, they do all jobs that a business

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.280
requires.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:33.689
Thus the business fails because the technician
burns out, unable to juggle all the jobs that

00:05:33.689 --> 00:05:37.330
need doing and they ultimately despair.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.880
The solution according to Gerber is not necessarily
to turn from being an entrepreneur to being

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.789
a technician but to unleash the entrepreneur
spirit within the technician.

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:47.569
The technician who goes into business without
an entrepreneur perspective will fail.

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:49.479
There is no argument about this.

00:05:49.479 --> 00:05:53.190
You can still be a technician and hard working,
loving what you do, but you need to be an

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:56.590
entrepreneur, too and an entrepreneur is someone
who knows that a business is not simply a

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.360
place to go to work, it is much more than
that.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.360
So what does that mean?

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.360
I had a conversation.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.819
One of my good friends finally came to me
and said, "Look, I was working for a company.

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:05.819
I was the #7 guy.

00:06:05.819 --> 00:06:07.500
Then I became the #5 guy.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.219
Then I became the #3 guy.

00:06:09.219 --> 00:06:10.759
And then I became the #2 guy.

00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:12.999
And then I said, "I'm going to go out there
and be the #1 guy.

00:06:12.999 --> 00:06:16.240
He went and became the #1 guy and it was the
worst victory of his lifetime.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.650
He made 6 million here, 3 million here, 2
million here, 8 million here.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:23.949
He made nothing on the last one.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:24.949
No victory.

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:29.730
And he said that after realizing why that
was, I realized I don't make a good #1, but

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.840
I make a very good #2.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.620
And he repositioned himself and followed it
with another big victory.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:36.620
Why is this?

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.419
He understood himself.

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:41.919
That's what E Myth will do when you read it,
getting a whole different perspective in your

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:42.919
business.

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:45.580
#8: The next book I want to tell you about
is again by Robert Greene.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:54.770
The 40 Laws of Power was #12 on my list and
I put Mastery at #8 and I'll tell you why

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.849
I put Mastery at #8.

00:06:57.849 --> 00:07:01.449
So many people in life will live a life.

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:04.590
. . let's just say that you and I have 75
to 100 years to live.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:09.129
Some of you live 75, some may live 92.

00:07:09.129 --> 00:07:12.270
Most people die never really mastering anything.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.710
They master being bored.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.569
They master watching T.V.

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:22.319
They master a lot of other things, but very
few people master a skill set or a trade.

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:23.879
Very few people.

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:24.969
And I don't care what you do.

00:07:24.969 --> 00:07:29.229
I saw a video the other day, I posted it on
Facebook, that was about these people who

00:07:29.229 --> 00:07:32.379
are taking these things and they're spinning
it and it's going around and they're doing

00:07:32.379 --> 00:07:36.659
it so perfectly and this one guy who's clean
the window at an airport which was insane.

00:07:36.659 --> 00:07:39.939
This other guys that's cutting the watermelon
in a way that's just ridiculous.

00:07:39.939 --> 00:07:43.309
This other guys that cutting an onion in a
way I've never seen before.

00:07:43.309 --> 00:07:44.629
And I respect that.

00:07:44.629 --> 00:07:46.499
I respect professionals.

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:51.279
I respect people that take a certain small
thing and they become masters at it.

00:07:51.279 --> 00:07:57.499
This book does a very good job using stories
of whether it's Franklin or some other musicians

00:07:57.499 --> 00:08:02.550
on explaining how you, yourself, can go from
somebody who's a novice at anything you do

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:03.819
that no one knows about.

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:07.919
You may like it but you're not good at it
or even great at it, and using formulas that

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:12.930
other people have used for you to become a
master at what you're doing.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.229
Lots of people, they see opportunities and
they say, "Oh my God, I want to do this.

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:21.069
Oh, I'm going to go out there and do this
or that" and they keep changing, changing,

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:24.680
changing, over a 5, 10, 15, 20 year period,
and they never really experience a big victory

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.659
because they just learned a lot of things
but they never mastered anything.

00:08:28.659 --> 00:08:31.460
Very, very important book.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:35.760
One of the main reasons why people kept asking
me, you know, someone told me the other day,

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.229
"Pat, I see you more as a technology guy,
man.

00:08:39.229 --> 00:08:40.820
I see you as a software guy.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:43.100
I see you as a Silicon Valley guy.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:44.100
Seriously?

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.600
Insurance and financial services?

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.540
Don't you think that's boring?

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:50.200
Don't you think you should have gone into
Silicone Valley and worked as some kind of

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.320
a technology guy?" Have I thought about it?

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:53.770
Yes, I've thought about it.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:55.930
But I chose this industry.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:58.980
And I was already into it too deep where I
fell in love with it and I said, "We've got

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.010
to figure out a way to take this to a whole
different level.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:05.180
And now we're disrupting the industry in a
way that others aren't doing, right?

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:06.380
But I chose this [the book, Mastery].

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:10.320
This became a very big commitment that I have
to myself and I think you can do it as well.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.240
#7: The next book.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:17.940
As an entrepreneur you will have a lot of
conflicts you'll have to face.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:23.150
One great book that I have on my list is,
not on this list but on my top 100 list is

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:27.540
The 5 Dysfunctions of a Team by Patrick Lencioni,
but it's not the next book.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.770
And he talks about one of the levels that
people have a hard time with is avoidance

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:31.770
of conflict.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.100
A lot of people don't even want to deal with
conflict.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:37.930
We're afraid of conflict because you had a
father or mother or an ex or somebody that

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:40.350
you know, you would have a fight and it was
always ugly, and screaming and your mom were

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:44.580
screaming and your dad, and people didn't
speak for two or three weeks, and so every

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:46.000
time conflict comes up, you run away.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.000
No, no, no, no.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.700
It's okay, no worries, let's work it, let's
move on, right?

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.400
But it doesn't really improve things.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:57.570
This book make it on my # 7 list, Crucial
Conversations, which has sold 2 million copies.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:58.730
It's written by these four consultants.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.650
They wrote the book together.

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:04.410
I've gone to a course of theirs; I highly
recommend it, it's a very good course.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:07.620
We even made a funny video about it, I think
2 years ago, it's a really funny video we

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.820
made where Mario and Paul are actually acting
with gummy bears and all this stuff.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.120
Real, real interesting on what happened with
that.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:19.220
I like this book because you're going to have
to have tough conversations with vendors,

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:23.770
with partners, with employees, with staff,
with executives, with sales, with media, with

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:25.500
many different people.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.610
How you go about doing it could cost you relationships,
could cost you negative feedback, could cost

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.251
you a person that doesn't want to do business
with you.

00:10:32.251 --> 00:10:33.930
It could cost you losing a good client.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:38.310
It could cost you many different things, so
it's a very, very important skill set.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:42.210
Don't watch these movies and people say, "Well,
Steve Jobs cursed everybody out.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:45.810
You know, I just saw Ashton Kutcher act like
Steve Jobs was cursing everybody out.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.070
I'm going to take that approach."

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:48.070
Okay, great.

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:50.430
try doing that when you're negotiating with
people where the leverage is not on your side.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:51.560
Then what are you going to do?

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.880
Well Mark Zuckerberg in Social Network, the
movie - did you see how he spoke to the attorney?

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.600
He learned how to do some of these things.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:58.740
That doesn't happen by pure luck.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.260
You have to learn that skill set as well.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:03.830
So let me read you a part of it here, in the
book that it helps you with.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:04.830
Look what it says.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:06.390
"Some common crucial conversations.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.590
You tell me if you some of these areas you
can improve in.

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:11.950
Ending a relationship -- how to go about doing
it.

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:15.880
Talking to a coworker that behaves offensively
or makes suggestive comments.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:17.240
Asking a friend to repay a loan.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:20.459
If you have a friend that needs to repay a
loan, it tells you how to do it in here.

00:11:20.459 --> 00:11:22.820
Giving the boss feedback about her behavior.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:27.200
Approaching a boss who's breaking his own
safety or quality policies.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:29.280
Critiquing a colleagues work.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:30.910
Asking a roommate to move out.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:35.000
One of our friends could probably use that,
but we're not going to get into that.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.140
Resolving custody or visitation issues with
an ex spouse.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:40.220
Holy moly, some people need help with that.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.390
Dealing with a rebellious teen.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:44.320
Talking to a team member that isn't keeping
commitments.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.110
Discussing problems with sexual intimacy.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.110
Wow!

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:48.110
That's deep.

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.390
Confronting a loved one about substance abuse
problems.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.750
A lot of people have that issue as well.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:55.410
Talking to a colleague who's hoarding information
or resources.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.550
Giving an unfavorable performance review.

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:00.820
Asking in-laws to quit interfering.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:02.970
Any married people can relate to that.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:05.730
Talking to a coworker about a personal hygiene
problem.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:06.730
Hello.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:09.750
Have you ever had any of these issues that
you didn't know how to handle?

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:11.200
Go buy this book.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:12.790
Crucial Conversations.

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:15.530
#6 is a book that hasn't done well.

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.430
It hasn't sold that many copies.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:22.440
I even told the author - -I hired him to come
from New York to Palm Springs at that one

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:23.440
event we had.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:28.010
We rented a 17,000 square foot house, it was
a mastermind, it was the most epic mastermind

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:32.290
ever with a few special moments that we had.

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:36.640
One with John Mason, a buddy of ours that
is the worst basketball player in the world,

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:41.260
ended up winning the game of 21 that lasted
two and a half hours in 115 degree temperature.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:42.630
He closed his eyes.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:46.600
He prayed to all the gods in the universe,
left handed shot the ball, it went in.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:48.720
It's the only shot he made, but he did end
up winning the game.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:52.850
Anyways, long story short, I brought in this
guy, and I said, "Howard, man, why didn't

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:54.990
this book do better?"

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:56.490
This book I got purely by luck.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:59.940
You know how when you buy a book on Amazon
and then all of a sudden other books come

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.870
up and by pure luck you hit something.

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:05.490
I searched this title and it came up and I
bought it.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:06.490
Nobody recommended the book to me.

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:08.150
I found this book by pure luck.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:11.610
If they do a better job with this book they'll
sell so many more copies.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:17.190
It's a book by Howard Guttman called Great
Business Teams: Cracking the Code for Standout

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:18.250
Performance.

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:20.130
Maybe we need a better title for that book.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:21.290
It's a phenomenal book.

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:24.850
Don't let this book cover, title, all this
stuff fool you.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:26.230
It's an exceptional book.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:30.130
Look, there's a part of it here in this book
where it talks about how to hire people and

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:31.130
how to hold them accountable.

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.760
How to go through a performance, how to do
the one-on-one critiquing.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:35.210
How to create a culture.

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:40.480
How to overcome people, to know how to deal
with conflict and collaborate instead of compromise,

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:42.230
instead of avoidance, instead of arguing.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:44.070
It goes through every single thing.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.620
And one of the parts is accountability.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:50.370
And it gives you step-by-step on how to hold
your team members accountable.

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:52.660
No one likes accountability, and it explains
how to do it.

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.630
Point 1, be open and candid.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:55.630
Say what's on your mind.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:58.270
Number 2, play for the team, not yourself
or your function.

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:00.170
Number 3, act like you know it or be engaged.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:01.880
Add value, keep the team on track.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:03.580
Number 4, be personally accountable, deliver
on promises.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:04.660
Number 5, meet deadlines.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:07.650
Number 6, Hold one another accountable for
delivering business results.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:11.410
Number 7, Hold one another accountable for
resolving interpersonal conflicts.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:14.330
Number 8, be receptive to feedback from others.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:18.140
Listen carefully, depersonalize and change
your behavior accordingly.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:21.360
Number 9, base feedback to others on observable
behavior.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:25.370
Deliver feedback objectively without personalizing
it or blaming.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:32.150
Number 9, Act as a coach to team members in
need and number 10, observe the protocols

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:36.000
and ground rules by which the team has agreed
to operate.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.880
Lots of great value in this book.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:39.420
I highly, highly recommend this.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:42.150
This book made the #6 list for me.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:43.290
Great Business Teams.

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:47.700
#5: Next one is a book I read 13 years ago.

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:49.350
It was a book I couldn't put down.

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:50.350
It's somewhat technical.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:59.050
Is the #5 book on my list and that book is
called, "Power vs. Force" by David R. Hawkins.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:02.400
This book has been recommended by Wayne Dyer,
Oprah Winfrey, many different people.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.070
A lot of people love this book.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:05.470
And what it talks about is this.

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:08.160
A lot of times in business you'll try to force
people to move.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.210
You'll try to force people to do things.

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:14.640
You'll try to force things to happen in your
business and you feel like you're absolutely

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:16.720
burning out because you're forcing it.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:21.770
It explains how to go from force to power
and influence.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:26.690
There's a part in this book which explains
the different level of consciousness.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:31.920
I did a video on this I think four years ago
that had to do with Star Wars.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.780
And I went through the different levels of
consciousness and it explains the different

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:38.150
levels of consciousness from the lowest to
the highest level.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:39.280
Lowest level is shame.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:41.410
You're ashamed of everything you've done.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:46.980
Guilt, then apathy, then grief, fear, then
desire, desire, like for sex or drugs or alcohol,

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:48.870
then anger, then pride.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:53.300
The it's equilibrium, which is the first level
of consciousness which is a positive one.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:55.830
Courage, you have the courage to talk about
your faults.

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:57.020
You have the courage to improve.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:00.590
You have the courage to get up and say, this
is what I haven't been doing, I'm going to

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:01.590
improve on this.

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:02.670
You have the courage to risk.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:03.890
You have the courage to do things.

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:05.160
You have the courage to lead.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:08.990
Then it's neutrality, you can sit there and
take other people's opinions and you can be

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:09.990
neutral.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:12.230
Then it's willingness, you are willing to
make things work.

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:14.710
You are willing to rekindle relationships,
you are willing to forgive.

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:17.010
You are willing to work on improving.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:21.480
Then it's acceptance, accepting people and
not judging people's way, political beliefs,

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:26.350
religious beliefs, you know the way they view
certain things, you have a certain way of

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:27.350
accepting that.

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:28.350
Reason.

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:29.760
You become good at processing issues.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.810
You become a source where people will come
to you to reason different issues.

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:33.810
Then it's love.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:34.810
You learn to love people.

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:35.810
That's 500.

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:36.810
Then it's joy.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:37.810
You enjoy life.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:39.661
Then it's peace, not a lot gets to you.

00:16:39.661 --> 00:16:43.570
Then the highest level is enlightenment, which
only a handful of people have reached that

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:46.050
level, 700-1000 is what he talks about.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:47.050
I just love this book.

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:51.850
It explains, a part in the book where he says
about this phenomenon, that when a person

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:57.680
gets to the phase of working, elevation consciousness,
often inspires a prolonged transcendence of

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:00.580
pain and exhaustion necessary to achieve higher
levels of performance.

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:04.560
He says that this phenomenon is commonly described
in terms of pushing oneself to the point where

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:08.529
one suddenly breaks through a performance
barrier and the activity becomes effortless.

00:17:08.529 --> 00:17:12.339
The body seems to move with grace, and ease
of its own accord.

00:17:12.339 --> 00:17:14.799
It's like mystical, it's magical.

00:17:14.799 --> 00:17:19.559
As though animated by some invisible force,
the accompanying state of joy's quite distinct

00:17:19.559 --> 00:17:20.660
from the thrill of success.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:23.779
It's a joy of peace and oneness with all that
lives.

00:17:23.779 --> 00:17:26.370
You get to a point when you're running a business
that everything becomes effortless.

00:17:26.370 --> 00:17:28.630
People say, "How does he make it look so easy?"

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.129
Because it really is easy.

00:17:30.129 --> 00:17:34.880
I went through a certain wall to get to this
point where everything becomes easy.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:40.029
Phenomenal book, don't let the book's technical-ness,
that's the word, in the first 40-50 pages

00:17:40.029 --> 00:17:41.850
fool you to not finish this book.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:43.690
It's a very good book.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:47.740
#4: This is another book like Howard Guttman's
book.

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:51.539
I contacted the author and said, "Hey, how
come this book didn't sell a half a million

00:17:51.539 --> 00:17:52.539
copies?

00:17:52.539 --> 00:17:54.030
I mean, did you guys do any marketing with
this book?

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:58.490
I actually wanted to buy his book, meaning
the rights to his book, because I was fascinated

00:17:58.490 --> 00:17:59.490
with his book.

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:01.039
You probably never heard of it.

00:18:01.039 --> 00:18:08.440
The book is called, "Barbarians to Bureaucrats:Corporate
Life Cycle Strategies."

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:13.620
And I loved this book because what he talks
about is what happens to societies.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:15.340
It's a similar thing that happens to companies.

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:18.749
Remember how earlier I talked about to look
at your company like a country?

00:18:18.749 --> 00:18:22.460
He talks about a country is made by somebody
that goes out there and takes the territory

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:26.460
and then boom, there's warriors, barbarians,
and then there's explorers, asking where's

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:28.039
the lake, where's this.

00:18:28.039 --> 00:18:30.740
You know, then there's the builders, "let's
build a building."And then there's administrators,

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:31.740
"We need some laws."

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:35.080
And then there are the guys that come late
and they're like, "I know everything because

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:38.330
I'm the educated, I'm smart"and everything
collapses, right?

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:39.510
You see this happening in countries.

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:42.580
We have that happening a little bit in America
right now.

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:43.669
But it's a fascinating book.

00:18:43.669 --> 00:18:47.059
Then you have a synergist that comes and gets
everybody together.

00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:48.249
So it explains that phase.

00:18:48.249 --> 00:18:50.840
The first phase, the prophet, the visionary.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:54.809
Then barbarian, then builder, explore, then
administrator, then a bureaucrat, than aristocrat,

00:18:54.809 --> 00:18:56.899
then you have the synergist.

00:18:56.899 --> 00:19:05.909
There's a part of the book that I want to
read to you on how he views a prophet.

00:19:05.909 --> 00:19:06.980
A prophet is the visionary.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.481
I'll just give you an idea to see how he talks
about it.

00:19:09.481 --> 00:19:15.419
If you work for a prophet, don't expect them
to provide specific objectives or instructions.

00:19:15.419 --> 00:19:18.620
He is more likely to send you out to vague
missions.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:22.690
Ask him to discuss your objectives, then write
your own based on your discussion and provide

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:24.179
him with a copy.

00:19:24.179 --> 00:19:26.470
Don't expect him to follow up on the details
of your work.

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:27.470
He doesn't care.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:31.649
Talk to him about the large goals toward which
you are working and how those goals fit in

00:19:31.649 --> 00:19:33.019
to his vision.

00:19:33.019 --> 00:19:37.820
Seek out the prophet for advice and ideas,
particularly on large and visionary issues.

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:42.669
Every prophet values thinking and believes
that others who enjoy discussing the ideas

00:19:42.669 --> 00:19:45.029
are also among the wise.

00:19:45.029 --> 00:19:47.309
Be tolerant of his latest idea.

00:19:47.309 --> 00:19:51.799
It may sound crazy, impractical and a complete
change in direction.

00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:56.649
Don't confront him with all this that is wrong
with his brainstorm.

00:19:56.649 --> 00:19:59.220
He is sensitive and doesn't want to have his
ideas squelched.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.600
I mean, this is just fascinating.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:03.950
If you work with a prophet, this makes sense.

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:06.809
Now, if a prophet works for you, you are lucky.

00:20:06.809 --> 00:20:08.649
Recognize him with his creative abilities.

00:20:08.649 --> 00:20:10.929
Reinforce and encourage those talents.

00:20:10.929 --> 00:20:14.690
Do not demand that he be well-organized or
conform to standard procedures.

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:16.769
He needs you to listen.

00:20:16.769 --> 00:20:20.100
He needs to know that his visionary ideas
are important to you.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:24.110
Let him know that within your company there's
room and opportunity for the implementation

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:25.340
of his ideas.

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:28.200
Help him distinguish between his regular job
and his creative ideas.

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:31.289
He may need to justify his salary and mundane
work.

00:20:31.289 --> 00:20:32.659
Protect him from bureaucrats.

00:20:32.659 --> 00:20:33.750
Holy moly.

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:38.500
Remember that in mature companies, prophets
are all too often crucified.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:39.500
How interesting is that?

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:40.879
I've experienced that before.

00:20:40.879 --> 00:20:42.080
Have patience.

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:46.480
Prophets work not for this quarter's results,
but for the impact they can have over the

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:47.480
long run.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:49.009
They're long-term thinkers.

00:20:49.009 --> 00:20:55.150
Their view is very long-range and insistence
on immediate results destroys creativity.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:56.150
So why is that so important?

00:20:56.150 --> 00:20:59.770
Well, you've got to also learn about how the
barbarian is, how the builder is, explorer

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:02.840
is, administrator, bureaucrats, aristocrats.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:05.340
Then whatever environment you're working in,
you're going to sit there and say, "That guy's

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:06.429
an aristocrat."

00:21:06.429 --> 00:21:07.570
And you'll know how to deal with him.

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:12.230
"That guy's a builder and explorer, that's
what we need."

00:21:12.230 --> 00:21:13.230
This is unbelievable!

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:14.720
And you'll learn.

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:17.990
Phenomenal book, Lawrence Miller, if you're
watching this, great job writing this book.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:22.690
I highly suggest you try to figure out a rewrite
a new one, I'll be one of the first to promote

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:24.799
your new book if you do.

00:21:24.799 --> 00:21:26.470
So you've got a big fan here with your book.

00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:27.789
#3:Alright, next one here.

00:21:27.789 --> 00:21:28.789
It's a classic.

00:21:28.789 --> 00:21:30.840
The next one is the first book I ever read.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:33.350
It's a book my sister told me to read.

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:35.980
When I worked at Bally Total Fitness, she
said, "You need to read this book."

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:39.289
I did and it changed the way that I treated
people, and viewed people.

00:21:39.289 --> 00:21:42.649
Because at the end of the day, you've got
to know your #1 product is people.

00:21:42.649 --> 00:21:43.649
The #1 product.

00:21:43.649 --> 00:21:48.009
It's not software, it's not demo products,
it's not pharmaceuticals, it's not real estate,

00:21:48.009 --> 00:21:50.720
it's not social media, it's not marketing,
it's not advertising.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:51.720
It's people.

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:56.480
And if you don't know your #1 product is people,
you're going to have problems.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:58.629
So, the #3 book is How to Win Friends and
Influence People.

00:21:58.629 --> 00:21:59.629
It's a fascinating book.

00:21:59.629 --> 00:22:00.629
It's a classic.

00:22:00.629 --> 00:22:01.629
Dale Carnegie.

00:22:01.629 --> 00:22:02.629
I don't know how many copies have sold.

00:22:02.629 --> 00:22:04.909
The last number I read was 45 million.

00:22:04.909 --> 00:22:07.570
It's one of those books that you need to read.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:12.070
In a nutshell, at the end, he talks about
the different way of thinking to winning people.

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:14.520
Here's what he says: "Principle #1.

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:17.570
The only way to get the best of an argument
is to avoid it.

00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:18.570
#2.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:19.970
Show respect for other's opinions.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:21.029
Never say "your wrong."

00:22:21.029 --> 00:22:23.749
#3: If you are wrong, admit it quickly and
emphatically.

00:22:23.749 --> 00:22:26.200
#4: Begin in a friendly way.

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:30.179
#5: Get the other person saying "yes, yes,
" immediately.

00:22:30.179 --> 00:22:33.409
#6:Let the other person do a great deal of
talking.

00:22:33.409 --> 00:22:37.380
#7: Let the other person feel like the idea
is his or hers.

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:41.130
#8:Try honestly to see things from the other
person's point of view.

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:44.950
#9: Be sympathetic with the other person's
ideas and desires.

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:47.320
#10: Appeal to the noble motives.

00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:49.129
#11: Dramatize your ideas.

00:22:49.129 --> 00:22:50.749
#12: Throw down a challenge."

00:22:50.749 --> 00:22:51.990
Read the book Very good book.

00:22:51.990 --> 00:22:55.769
It's probably one of those books you want
to read once a year, or listen to the audio

00:22:55.769 --> 00:22:56.769
once a year.

00:22:56.769 --> 00:22:59.149
If you read it once, just keep listening to
the audio every year, one time.

00:22:59.149 --> 00:23:01.059
It will help you out a lot.

00:23:01.059 --> 00:23:05.039
#2 is an interesting book that was recommended
to me by a good friend of mine Bryon.

00:23:05.039 --> 00:23:08.370
Byron, if you're watching this, I don't know
how many people I've had buy this book.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:10.429
It's just a fascinating book.

00:23:10.429 --> 00:23:15.460
Byron and I, I don't know if we were in Dallas,
Byron, or if we were in Orlando.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:17.520
We were both invited to this nice dinner.

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:22.370
Byron runs a very, very big online insurance
company that does very well.

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:27.640
He's very well off, he has a son that's extremely
successful who was living in. . . he took

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:32.129
over Shaq's last house in L.A. and does very
